Moving your state file from your local folder (local backend) to a remote backend in AWS—specifically an S3 bucket for state storage and a DynamoDB table for state locking—is the industry best practice.

🌟 Why This is Best Practice
Prevents Corruption (State Locking): DynamoDB prevents two people (or two CI/CD runs) from running terraform apply at the exact same time.

Security & Backup: S3 allows you to enable versioning (so you can rollback if state gets corrupted) and encryption at rest.

Team Collaboration: Everyone on your team accesses the single, up-to-date state in AWS instead of passing a local terraform.tfstate file around.

🛠️ Step-by-Step Migration Guide
Step 1: Create the S3 Bucket & DynamoDB Table
You need an S3 bucket and DynamoDB table to host your state before configuring the backend. You can create them in your main.tf file first, or via CLI.

Add this block to your root main.tf to let Terraform create the remote backend resources for you:

Terraform
# S3 Bucket for state storage
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "tf_state" {
  bucket        = "abdul-tf-state-storage-2026" # Must be globally unique
  force_destroy = false
}

# Enable versioning on state bucket (Critical for backups)
resource "aws_s3_bucket_versioning" "tf_state_versioning" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.tf_state.id
  versioning_configuration {
    status = "Enabled"
  }
}

# DynamoDB table for state locking
resource "aws_dynamodb_table" "tf_locks" {
  name         = "terraform-state-locks"
  billing_mode = "PAY_PER_REQUEST"
  hash_key     = "LockID"

  attribute {
    name = "LockID"
    type = "S"
  }
}
Run terraform apply to provision these resources locally first.

Step 2: Configure the backend Block
Once the bucket and DynamoDB table exist in AWS, add a backend "s3" block inside your terraform {} configuration in main.tf:

Terraform
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0"

  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "abdul-tf-state-storage-2026"
    key            = "global/s3/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-west-2"
    dynamodb_table = "terraform-state-locks"
    encrypt        = true
  }
}
Step 3: Run terraform init to Migrate State
Run terraform init in your terminal. Terraform will automatically detect that you're switching from local state to a remote S3 backend and prompt you:

PowerShell
terraform init
Terraform will output:

Do you want to copy existing state to the new backend?
Enter a value: yes

Type yes and hit Enter.

Step 4: Verify Local File Cleanup & Verification
Check your S3 bucket in the AWS Console — you will see global/s3/terraform.tfstate uploaded!

Your local terraform.tfstate file will now be empty or ignored by Terraform operations.

Add terraform.tfstate and terraform.tfstate.backup to your .gitignore file so you never accidentally commit state to source control.

🧪 Test the New Setup
Run a plan to ensure Terraform seamlessly reads from S3:

PowerShell
terraform plan
You should see: No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration. managed via your new remote state!